{
 "lead": "Taylor's theorem replaces a smooth function near a point by a polynomial plus a small error term. The polynomial is built from the function's derivatives at that point, so a computer can evaluate e<sup>0.1</sup>, sin 0.2 or log 1.05 using only addition and multiplication. Engineers use it for linearisation, error control in numerical software, and small-angle approximations in physics. Maclaurin's theorem is the special case where the centre is zero. The remainder tells how wrong the polynomial is.",
 "prereq": "You need derivatives and the mean value theorems from <a href=\"#derivative-as-a-function-and-differentiability\">Chapter 1.3</a> and <a href=\"#rolle-s-theorem-and-mean-value-theorems\">Chapter 1.4</a>.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Taylor polynomial at a point",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Choose a centre a and a whole number n. The Taylor polynomial of degree n at a uses the value and the first n derivatives of f at a. It is the best polynomial of that degree for matching f close to a."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "P<sub>n</sub>(x) = Σ<sub>k=0</sub><sup>n</sup> [f<sup>(k)</sup>(a) / k!] (x − a)<sup>k</sup>",
     "where": "P<sub>n</sub>(x) is the Taylor polynomial; f<sup>(k)</sup>(a) is the k-th derivative of f at a, with f<sup>(0)</sup>(a) = f(a); k! = 1×2×…×k and 0! = 1; a is the centre; x is the point near a."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The theorem writes f(x) = P<sub>n</sub>(x) + R<sub>n</sub>(x). The remainder R<sub>n</sub>(x) is the error left after the polynomial. When a = 0 the polynomial is called the Maclaurin polynomial. For example, the degree 2 Maclaurin polynomial of e<sup>x</sup> is 1 + x + x²/2."
    },
    {
     "type": "bullets",
     "items": [
      "P<sub>1</sub> matches value and slope at a: the tangent line.",
      "P<sub>2</sub> also matches curvature at a.",
      "Higher n matches more derivatives, so the error usually shrinks near a."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Lagrange remainder and error bounds",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The Lagrange form of the remainder gives the error as one extra derivative evaluated at an unknown point c. You do not need the exact c. You bound the derivative over the interval between a and x."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "R<sub>n</sub>(x) = [f<sup>(n+1)</sup>(c) / (n+1)!] (x − a)<sup>n+1</sup>",
     "where": "c is some point between a and x; f<sup>(n+1)</sup>(c) is the next derivative after the polynomial's last derivative; (n+1)! is the factorial of n+1; |x − a| is the distance from the centre."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "To bound the error, find the largest possible |f<sup>(n+1)</sup>(c)| on that interval, call it M. Then |R<sub>n</sub>(x)| ≤ M |x − a|<sup>n+1</sup> / (n+1)!."
    },
    {
     "type": "steps",
     "items": [
      "Identify f, a, n and x.",
      "Write the Lagrange remainder using c between a and x.",
      "Bound the next derivative on that interval by a number M.",
      "Substitute M, |x − a| and (n+1)! into the bound."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Standard Maclaurin expansions",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "These expansions are worth memorising for small x. The factorial grows fast, so low-degree polynomials often give good accuracy near zero. Signs alternate in sin, cos and log(1+x)."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Function",
      "Maclaurin expansion",
      "Valid range for the series"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "e<sup>x</sup>",
       "1 + x + x²/2! + x³/3! + …",
       "all x"
      ],
      [
       "sin x",
       "x − x³/3! + x⁵/5! − …",
       "all x"
      ],
      [
       "cos x",
       "1 − x²/2! + x⁴/4! − …",
       "all x"
      ],
      [
       "log(1+x)",
       "x − x²/2 + x³/3 − …",
       "−1 < x ≤ 1"
      ],
      [
       "(1+x)<sup>n</sup>",
       "1 + nx + n(n−1)x²/2! + …",
       "|x| < 1 for general n"
      ]
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "For small x, sin x ≈ x and cos x ≈ 1 − x²/2. These are the small-angle approximations used in pendulums and AC circuit phasors."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
